{"author":{"id":"1d5f412cdeafe9987ad9876b0995bf5032718543d34c038a50cdb41808e9874c"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788305210,"nonce":"kntxAfi6y2gtDMBZFIMKFGgJiAA=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOULVc1M8AAAABPPiJDg","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/torram-ng/issues/27","origin":"github"},"title":"Hashing is single-threaded","message":"All hashing happens on one thread, so throughput is capped at roughly 2.3 GB/s on a core with SHA-NI, and far lower without it.\n\nThat cap only matters on fast storage. Rough shape of the problem:\n\n| Storage | Sequential read | Bottleneck |\n|---|---|---|\n| HDD | 150-250 MB/s | disk, by ~10x |\n| SATA SSD | ~550 MB/s | disk, by ~4x |\n| NVMe Gen3 | ~3.5 GB/s | hashing |\n| NVMe Gen4/5 | 5-14 GB/s | hashing, badly |\n\nSo concurrency helps on NVMe and is worthless — or harmful — on a rotational disk, where concurrent reads cause seek thrashing.\n\nTwo things worth stating for whoever implements this:\n\n- **Threads are sufficient.** `hashlib` releases the GIL for buffers of 2048 bytes and larger, so SHA-1 genuinely runs in parallel across threads. Multiprocessing would add IPC copies of multi-megabyte buffers to solve a problem that does not exist.\n- **Concurrency is a property of the device, not the process.** Sources should be grouped by `st_dev`, with the reader count chosen per device (1 for rotational, more for NVMe; `/sys/block/*/queue/rotational` on Linux), and the whole thing configurable so it can be measured rather than guessed.","files":null}]}